% ============================================================================
% green-signal-permitted-movement — the individual movements a driver facing
% a steady CIRCULAR GREEN signal is permitted to make, as the MUTCD's own
% sentence enumerates them (adj-facts-stdlib, TRANSPORTATION).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `traffic-lights.adj`
% (`traffic_light_meaning(color, meaning)`, green → the single atomic value
% `proceed`). That table's own header already quotes, VERBATIM, the MUTCD
% sentence for green — and that SAME sentence enumerates FOUR distinct
% permitted movements, not just one undifferentiated "proceed":
%
%   "Vehicular traffic facing a CIRCULAR GREEN signal indication is
%    permitted to proceed straight through or turn right or left or make a
%    U-turn movement"
%
% The color-to-meaning schema collapses all four into `proceed`; this
% sibling gives each movement its own row. Recall is a plain listing query
% (there is no key column — all four movements are simultaneously true for
% green, unlike a keyed lookup):
%
%     ? green_signal_permitted_movement($Movement)
%       % straight_through ; turn_right ; turn_left ; u_turn
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `green_signal_permitted_movement(movement)` carrying the
% citation, so the query above is the ordinary SLD recall — the engine
% returns every listed movement WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero
% model calls. A movement the MUTCD sentence does NOT name — `reverse`, for
% instance — has no row, so a check for it fails rather than being invented.
%
% Provenance: the full MUTCD Section 4D.04 green sentence, re-extracted from
% the page and verified character-exactly against its RAW HTML. `trust
% authoritative` — the FHWA Manual on Uniform Traffic Control Devices is
% the federal standard for U.S. traffic control devices, the same tier
% `traffic-lights.adj` carries.
%
% *** THE CITATION WAS TRUNCATED UNTIL ISSUE #13916, AND THE WAY IT GOT
% THAT WAY IS WORTH KNOWING. *** It used to stop at "...or make a U-turn
% movement", omitting the clause the sentence actually ends with:
%
%     "... except as such movement is modified by lane-use signs, turn
%      prohibition signs, lane markings, roadway design, separate turn
%      signal indications, or other traffic control devices."
%
% Every row was therefore presented as an unconditional permission, backed
% by evidence with its qualifier cut off — the exact defect
% `sign-shape.adj` and `red-signal-permitted-movement.adj` both warn
% against in their own headers: A CITATION HAS TO CARRY THE QUALIFIER IT IS
% BEING USED TO JUSTIFY.
%
% The root cause was not carelessness about the text. This header used to
% say the span "reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME ... span already quoted
% inside `traffic-lights.adj`'s own header — NO NEW WebFetch". The quote
% was COPIED FROM A SIBLING LIBRARY'S PROSE rather than re-extracted from
% the source, so a truncation in that header propagated silently into a
% `source` envelope. A CITATION INHERITED FROM ANOTHER LIBRARY'S HEADER IS
% NOT VERIFIED PROVENANCE. Re-extract from the page, every time; the cost
% of one fetch is far below the cost of a citation that says less than the
% standard does.
%
% THE EXCEPTION STAYS IN THE CITATION RATHER THAN THE ATOMS, and the
% reason is NOT the one an earlier draft of this header gave. That draft
% called it a "whole-permission qualifier ... not any one movement", which
% the grammar does not support: the clause reads "except as SUCH MOVEMENT
% is modified", a singular distributive anaphor, and every device it names
% is movement-specific — a turn-prohibition sign prohibits a particular
% turn, a lane-use sign assigns particular movements to particular lanes.
% If anything it is MORE per-movement than the red sibling's exception,
% which is FRONTED and conditions the whole grant before stating it.
%
% The right reason is about what the qualifier DOES to a value, not where
% it sits:
%
%   - Red's "after stopping" is CONSTITUTIVE. It changes what the permitted
%     act IS, so a bare `turn_right` row would assert something red's
%     sentence never says. The qualifier has to be in the atom or the row
%     is false.
%   - Green's exception is DEFEASIBILITY. It can override any of the four
%     movements without changing what any of them IS, and it applies
%     uniformly to all four — so it carries NO row-discriminating
%     information. Pushed into the atoms it would decorate every row
%     identically while making none of them more accurate; kept in the
%     envelope it qualifies exactly what it qualifies.
%
% CONSTITUTIVE QUALIFIERS GO IN THE ATOM; DEFEASIBILITY GOES IN THE
% CITATION. That is the sharper statement of the rule `sign-shape.adj` and
% `karst-process-zone.adj` were already following.
%
% A NOTE ON THE RED SIBLING, since the two are meant to be read together.
% BOTH permissions are device-modifiable — green by this sentence's clause,
% red by its own "Except when a traffic control device is in place
% prohibiting a turn on red..." opening. FOR THE MOVEMENTS BOTH SIGNALS
% PERMIT, the difference is the stopping requirement. But red also permits
% STRICTLY FEWER MOVEMENTS: it allows neither `straight_through` nor
% `u_turn` at all, and its left turn is confined to one-way-street-into-
% one-way-street. An earlier draft of this header said the difference was
% "the stopping requirement and nothing else", which the red table's own
% rows refute — a claim worth getting right, since this header invites the
% reader to consult that table.
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table green_signal_permitted_movement {
    columns movement

    row (straight_through)
    row (turn_right)
    row (turn_left)
    row (u_turn)

    source "Vehicular traffic facing a CIRCULAR GREEN signal indication is permitted to proceed straight through or turn right or left or make a U-turn movement except as such movement is modified by lane-use signs, turn prohibition signs, lane markings, roadway design, separate turn signal indications, or other traffic control devices."
    locator "https://mutcd.fhwa.dot.gov/htm/2009/part4/part4d.htm"
    trust authoritative
}
